  "worked_solution": "Given a normal random variable $X\u223cN(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)=P(\\frac{a-\\mu }{\\sigma }<Z<\\frac{b-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z\u223cN(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =5$ and $\\sigma =10.$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-18.4<X<16.7) & =P(\\frac{-18.4-5}{10}<Z<\\frac{16.7-5}{10}) \\\\  & =P(-2.34<Z<1.17)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070250/assets/82e4ea3088176bbd9b03b7baa6aaa9389f21f9e9a286fa223d47bfae4252ccb6.png)\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $\u03a6(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-2.34<Z<1.17) & =P(Z<1.17)-P(Z\\le -2.34) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.17)-P(Z\\le -2.34) \\\\  & =\u03a6(1.17)-\u03a6(-2.34)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u03a6(-2.34) & =0.0096 \\\\ \u03a6(1.17) & =0.8790\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-2.34<Z<1.17) & =\u03a6(1.17)-\u03a6(-2.34) \\\\  & =0.8790-0.0096 \\\\  & =0.8694.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(-18.4<X<16.7)=0.8694.\n$$",
